WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.712
Integer overflow happens when an
arithmetic result cannot be represented in the

2
00:00:04.792 --> 00:00:06.031
intended type or range.

3
00:00:06.111 --> 00:00:08.898
In C, that concern is not isolated to
math-heavy code.

4
00:00:08.978 --> 00:00:11.889
Numeric values influence how much memory
is allocated,

5
00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:14.757
which element is accessed, how far a
parser advances,

6
00:00:14.837 --> 00:00:18.863
how many records are accepted, and whether
a resource limit has been reached.

7
00:00:18.943 --> 00:00:21.483
Integer mistakes are not only calculation
bugs.

8
00:00:21.563 --> 00:00:25.651
A wrong result can change a buffer length,
an array index, a loop count, a quota,

9
00:00:25.731 --> 00:00:29.633
a counter, a timestamp comparison, an
authorization-related threshold,

10
00:00:29.713 --> 00:00:30.766
or a resource limit.

11
00:00:30.846 --> 00:00:32.704
When later code trusts that result,

12
00:00:32.784 --> 00:00:36.934
the original arithmetic mistake can become
a reliability or security problem.

13
00:00:37.014 --> 00:00:41.164
C makes integer safety especially
important because behavior depends on type,

14
00:00:41.244 --> 00:00:45.828
signedness, width, conversion rules,
promotions, and the operation being performed.

15
00:00:45.908 --> 00:00:50.368
A value that appears reasonable before
arithmetic may become unsafe after addition,

16
00:00:50.448 --> 00:00:55.093
subtraction, multiplication, parsing,
shifting, or conversion into a different type.

17
00:00:55.173 --> 00:00:59.323
Defensive C code treats numeric values as
part of the program's trust boundary.

18
00:00:59.403 --> 00:01:03.677
Values from files, messages, APIs,
environment settings, user input, metadata,

19
00:01:03.757 --> 00:01:07.598
and even internal counters should be
checked before they control memory,

20
00:01:07.678 --> 00:01:10.465
indexing, loops, access decisions, or
resource use.

21
00:01:10.545 --> 00:01:14.943
This course focuses on prevention, review,
testing, and responsible engineering.

22
00:01:15.023 --> 00:01:16.943
We will look at the rules that matter,

23
00:01:17.023 --> 00:01:19.934
the failure patterns that reviewers should
recognize,

24
00:01:20.014 --> 00:01:24.164
and the practical habits that make integer
arithmetic safer before results are

25
00:01:24.244 --> 00:01:26.660
trusted by memory management or control
flow.
